TY  - THES
T1  - Three essays in regional economics
AN  - 1821929869
AB  - In the first essay, using state-level panel data from 1990 to 2011 and the difference-in-differences (DID) method, I find that implementation of Renewable Portfolio Standards (RPS) increases electricity prices when the RPS policy first becomes binding. It fills in the absence of empirical studies of the effect of RPS on electricity prices. In the second essay, using the spatial equilibrium growth model of Glaeser and Tobio (2008), I examine the patterns in U.S. regional population growth during the period of 2000 to 2010 across the natural amenity spectrum and the rural-urban continuum, and decompose growth into the portions attributable to variation in amenity demand, productivity, and elasticity of land supply. Among the results, across both metropolitan and nonmetropolitan areas, the variation in productivity dominated in terms of explaining the differences in population growth across the amenity spectrum, while the amenity rank was not positively related to population growth, nor strongly related to wage and housing cost growth across the states. While focusing solely on the growth patterns across nonmetropolitan areas, this study finds that increased amenity demand in natural amenity attractive areas still spurred their growth, although productivity effects continued to be important growth determinants. In the third essay, using micro-level data, I examine whether Texas has benefitted from its mix of public policies by estimating quality-adjusted wages and housing costs across the U.S. and examining their geographic patterns. The results for both metropolitan and nonmetropolitan areas together suggest that Texas had policies that caused it to be more productive but also less-amenity attractive to households relative to Oklahoma after controlling for other economic factors. In addition, capitalization of the productivity advantages into wages and housing costs and lower quality of life associated with the policies eliminated any previous policy-based growth advantages of Texas.
