TY  - THES
T1  - Essays in Applied Microeconomics
AN  - 1710772247
AB  - This dissertation collects two projects in applied microeconomics. The first chapter contributes to the literature on the labor supply of women in the formal market by investigating the impact of the provision of subsidized childcare on the labor supply of mothers of young children. The second chapter exploits evidence from a three years randomized controlled trial experiment to investigate the effect of market price information in rural Ghana on local farmers' ability to bargain for better prices from traders.     Chapter 1, "What Took You So Long? The Short and Longer-run Effects of Subsidized Child- care On Mothers' Labor Supply and Fertility" exploits inter-temporal and geographical variation in the rules for eligibility to enroll in public kindergarten to estimate the effect of access to this service on mothers' labor supply, fertility and schooling choices. While evidence exists that pro- viding subsidized childcare may increase mothers's participation to the labor force, little is known about the longer-run implications. This chapter fills the gap by providing evidence that mothers who have access to the subsidy are more likely to work not only in the short run but also in the next five years. This group of mothers is also less likely to have more children and on average gets higher wages, potentially because of increased experience.     Chapter 2, "Price Information, Inter-village Networks And "Bargaining Spillovers": Experi- mental Evidence From Ghana", is joint work with Nicole Hildebrandt, Yaw Nyarko and Giorgia Romagnoli. We study the short and medium-run impact of price information on the marketing outcomes of farmers in a remote area of rural Ghana using a two-year randomized evaluation of an SMS-based price alert service. The study makes two important contribution to the literature. First, by tracking the sales of each farmer in the study for two farming seasons, we are able to evaluate the medium-term treatments effects of the intervention as well as the dynamics over time. Second, by gathering data on communication networks across villages, we can examine the network externalities of the intervention. We show that the price alerts had a large positive effect (7%) on the price farmers received for yam during the first months. This effect faded away over time. Our empirical analysis suggests that this cannot be explained by a "fade out" of the salience of the price alerts nor to treatment group farmers sharing price information with the control group. Rather, our evidence suggests the presence of important network externalities on the bargaining strategies and outcomes of farmers and traders.
