TY  - THES
T1  - Three essays on labor markets, regulations and immigration in developing economies
AN  - 1696054981
AB  - In this dissertation I address issues related to labor markets, regulations and circular migration. In the first essay (with Tim Gindling and Juan Diego Trejos) we contribute to the literature of the impact of non-compliance on labor market outcomes in developing economies by evaluating the impact of the Campaña Nacional de Salarios Mínimos, designed by the Costa Rican government to increase compliance with minimum wages. Using a two-year panel data set of individuals we use a regression discontinuity approach and compare what happened to workers who before the Campaign had been earning below the minimum wage to those who had been earning above the minimum wage. We find that the Campaign led to an increase in compliance with minimum wages especially for women, younger, and less educated workers. We find no evidence that the Campaign had a negative impact on the employment of full-time workers.    In the second essay (with Tim Gindling and David Newhouse) we use the International Income Distribution Database to first estimate the earnings premium/penalty associated with self-employment in 73 countries around the world overtime. We than use these estimates to investigate the relationship between the regulatory environments measured through labor, business, credit and trade regulations and self-employment earnings differentials. Our findings do not provide support for the traditional hypothesis that labor market regulations lead to labor market segmentation. There is a more robust association between self-employment penalties and business, credit and trade regulations.    Using a household panel survey, the third essay attempts to fill a gap in the scarce South-South circular migration literature by identifying circular migrants in Nicaragua and exploring the correlation between individual and household characteristics and various forms of migration. The results show that circular migrants are distinct from other circular migrants. Compared with non-migrants, Nicaraguan circular migrants are predominately male, tend to be married, less likely to be poor, and have more years of education. This research is the first step in understanding South-South circular migration, and contributes to the formulation of future effective migration policy programs that extend beyond the agricultural industry and skilled labor and address unskilled labor in various industries.
